 hunch they didn't see the stimulus but  they just had a gut feeling that they  were going to get it right more often in  that target present interval so they bet  based on that gut feeling well when I  didn't show you though is that if we  change the question and we ask people to  simply indicate which target was more  visible regardless of whether they think  they got it right they show essentially  the same behavior there's no blind site  when you change the question either so  this means that when people are  confident in something even just a  little they are conscious of it even  just a little and vice versa so in  humans at least who have no neurological  deficits we have empirical evidence that  confidence is a sufficient indicator for  consciousness as well so the point is  that these two things confidence and  consciousness they're really very  connected and they're connected because  the circuitry underlying detection of an  object is what gets recruited for  metacognitive or introspective judgments  of certainty in that decision confidence  and consciousness are interconnected  because they rely on essentially the  same computational machinery and because  the introspective system is set up this  way you get these funny behaviors like  when something that looks a lot like  what you decided it is you're very  confident in your decision about its  identity because you discount any  aspects of it that might suggest it's  something else and this happens  especially when the reason it looks a  lot like what you decided it is is  because you've got a lot of noise in  your system like when we zapped you with  TMS and you have more noise than you  think you do  this can happen when we zap you with TMS  so we introduce a bunch of noise but it  could also happen that the perceptual  system underestimates the amount of  noise that it has for other reasons so  one example of this that I really like  is in the periphery of our vision so our  rods at the edge of our retinas are very  bad at detecting fine detail and they're  completely unable to detect color yet  what we subjectively experience in the  periphery is a whole lot more like this  picture on the left sorry on the right  than the picture on the left we don't  think we see only black and white and  fuzzy things in our visual periphery no  instead we see color we see fine detail  where we think we do we actually have an  inflated sense of confidence in the  color and the detail that we've  perceived and maybe the reason is that  the system has underestimated the noise  that occurs in the periphery of our  vision so others in the lab now are  investigating this type of subjective  inflation in the periphery so this  decision congruent evidence model really  does show how errors in the calculation  of confidence and over reliance on  decision congruent evidence that this  can really account for the nature of  subjective experience or awareness so  with that I'd like to thank all of my  collaborators without whom this work  would not have been possible and also  our funding sources and you all my  audience for your attention thank you  thank you dr. Peters for your  presentation in the Q&amp;A box found by  clicking on the green Q&amp;A button at the  lower left of the presentation window  and dr. Peters will answer as many  questions as we have time for today so  the questions excellent dr. Peters when  you  when you decoded decisions and  confidence to the location of the  information change in the brain this is  a really good question is is how the  representations of decision and versus  confidence do they look the same even  though the calculations look like  they're different so I think the best  way to answer this question is to show  you some work that we did on trying to  get at what those representations look  like in the brain and where they are and  when they arise so here on the upper  left of the graph is what I showed  earlier in the presentation but what we  can do from this decoding of the  decisions and the confidence judgments  is we can actually parcel ate the  information so that you can assign to  every electrode that we record from  across all of our participants how much  each electrode at each spot in the brain  contributes to our ability to decode so  to the extent that we can actually  decode decisions or confidence judgments  which electrodes are the important ones  to being able to do that decoding so now  if we look at the the lower part of the  graph here panel B you can see these are  the little dots represent all of the  electrodes that we have recorded from  all of our subjects and you can see that  on the top row of panel B that the  decision information really does seem to  rely more on these occipital electrodes  here especially between 200 to 600  milliseconds after the stimulus onset  and the representation looks a little  bit different for the confidence  judgments there are fewer red dots  overall but there are some more red dots  that appear here more in like the  parietal and even frontal regions and so  to get at this distribution of  information in a slightly different way  the next thing that we did was we can  group these electrodes by whether they  belong to one of the  for neocortical loads so the the frontal  lobe the parietal lobe the occipital  lobe and the temporal lobe and then we  can plot the average contribution of  electrodes that are in that neocortical  lobe as a function of time after the  stimulus onset and so that's what's  plotted up here in panel C and so you  can see that when we split it up by the  neocortical lobe that for decisions it's  really the occipital electrodes that are  doing the heavy lifting and to a little  bit of extent it's the parietal  electrodes to our sorry the temporal  electrodes but those frontal and  parietal electrodes especially early in  the trial period are not actually doing  as much of the heavy lifting they do  tend to tend to be more important later  and this pattern is really quite  different for the confidence judgments  so you do see this little occipital  spike here but it's later than the  occipital spikes that you see for the  decisions and it's not as strong and  it's not a sustained and then we also  see then that despite the fact that  during decisions occipital electrodes  are really winning they're contributing  the most for most of the Paris stimulus  window that here it actually switches  for the confidence judgments such that  first it's the occipital electrodes and  then it's the temporal electrodes that  are doing the heavy lifting so this  shows us that even though we can decode  the representations to a similar extent  for both decisions and confidence  judgments that the nature of these  representations is really quite  different and one more piece of  information that I can show you that the  representations are really very  different from each other is here so now  that we know how much each electrode  contributes to decoding each behavioral  factor we can see which are the most  important electrodes for decoding  decision and confidence judgments and  then we can see where those most  important electrodes are and see where  they overlap and so to the extent that  you do have electrodes that  significantly contribute to both  decisions and confidence judgments  they're pretty much in the occipital  region maybe a little bit in the  temporal region but if you look at the  y-axis over here you can see that  there's really not a lot of feature  overlaps so the electrodes that are  important for decoding decisions are  really not the same as the electrodes  and the features that are important for  decoding confidence judgments so I hope  that answers your question  maybe people had a gut feeling or a  hunch but still didn't actually see the  target and that's what made them able to  [Music]  write so um this is this this question I  actually get a lot when I talk about  this study so part of it can be answered  by the similarity between the answers  that participants gave when we asked  them to report their confidence versus  when they reported just the visibility  of the target so you'd think that if  they're doing the confidence judgment on  the basis of some gut feeling or hunch  that maybe they wouldn't be depending on  the same thing when we asked them about  visibility that that instead hopefully  what they're reporting is the actual  visual experience or visual qualia of  those stimulates but I think this also  can really be answered by the way that  we define blind sites the way that we  define subjective experience in this  task so what we're asking with this task  is here if I go to this slide what we're  asking with this task is if you can do  this task above chance when the target  is actually there  but doing so is introspectively or  subjectively no different from doing the  task when the target is physically not  there then you will be at 5050 and so  having a gut feeling or hunch in essence  there is something that it is like to  use philosophical language something  that it's like to have that gut feeling  or hunch that is subjectively different  from doing the task based on nothing and  so in that way we would say that these  people are not actually metacognitively  blind they do have some introspective  ability to do the task on the basis of  that gut feeling your hunch even if it's  not visual in nature this is a great  question  so to answer this question I'll show you  the stimuli that we use so we actually  made use of we made use of some data  that had been collected previously that  we're not having to do with this study  but they turned out to be very useful so  in the type of task that we use the  monkey is fixating on the screen and  then so here and we've we record from  the neurons and the superior colliculus  using a multi-channel deep probe and so  previous to the task you actually map  what's called the response field of the  superior colliculus neurons so this is  the area in the visual field that these  neurons really like and then a dot  motion stimulus comes on and then you  measure the way that the neurons respond  to this dot motion stimulus and the  stimuli that we were interested in were  cases where you've got a bunch of dots  moving in one direction or you've got a  bunch of dots moving in two directions  and it  the same percentage of dots moving in  one direction or the other so we can  call this preferred 100% preferred or  50/50 preferred and non-preferred  stimulate and so then we can measure how  these neurons fire in response to these  stimuli and so in panel C you can see  that when it's a hundred percent what we  call the preferred stimulus so this  indicates that the monkey should make a  choice  towards that response field that the  neurons really like that they fire a lot  more than when it's 50-50 preferred and  non-preferred so for each neuron then we  can calculate how much they change their  firing rate when there's only preferred  stimuli on the screen versus when  there's both preferred and non-preferred  stimuli on the screen and every neuron  changes its firing rate a little bit  differently and from that difference we  calculate a modulation index so the  higher the modulation index the more  normalized the neuron is the more it's  inhibited by neuro by other neurons that  have opposing tuning preferences and the  smaller the normalization index the less  normalized the unit is the closer to  zero it is the less normal as it is and  when we calculate this modulation index  on the basis of disjoint trial subsets  so we just split the trials into even  and odd trials and we calculate this  modulation index separately we see a  really nice correlation between the even  and odd trials so this shows that Tunes  normalization really does exist in the  superior colliculus and this is actually  the first report of two normalization  outside the cortex in a subcortical  structure and it's the first report of  change normalization in these  accumulation type units so we're really  excited about that  okay well thank you so much  dr. Peeters for your time today we truly  do appreciate it and that is all the  time we have for questions do you have  any final comments that you would like  to close today we're really I'd just  like to thank everybody for your  attention for listening to me talk about  my research it's very exciting to be  here and thank you very much to lab  roots also for putting this together and  invited me to participate well it's been  our pleasure to have you present  I'd like to thank the audience for  joining us today and for their  interesting questions we would like to  thank laberd's for today's educational  webcast this webcast can be viewed  on-demand through June 2017 labyrinths  will alert you via email when it is  available for replay we encourage you to  share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live events  i am christina mahalik with levers and  we'll see you here next time at laggards  comm good bye
 

